<%#
  ONE PUBLISHED PROJECT'S PAGE BODY (stage 3, S3-4; extracted stage 4, S4-4). Two callers render
  it: public/projects/show.html.erb (the visitor's page) and projects/preview.html.erb (the
  OWNER's preview of it, behind a banner, at any visibility — spec §7). Extracted rather than
  copied for the reason Maker::ProjectPhotos was: two surfaces claiming to show "what a visitor
  sees" that are two templates are a pair that drifts, and the drift is invisible until a maker
  publishes something the preview never showed them.

  IT READS `pub` AND NOTHING ELSE. No ivars, no session, no `current_user` — which is what lets the
  same markup render under Public::BaseController (ActionController::Base, no session at all) and
  under ProjectsController without a branch. Its `content_for` blocks stayed behind in show.html.erb
  on purpose: the noindex meta and the Atom <link> are the PUBLIC page's head, not the preview's.

  Local: pub (Maker::PublicProject).
%>
<% project = pub.project %>
<nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Project path">
  <%= link_to "@#{pub.handle}", public_user_path(pub.handle) %>
  <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
  <span aria-current="page"><%= project.name %></span>
</nav>

<div class="page-head public-head">
  <div class="page-title">
    <h1><%= project.name %></h1>
  </div>
  <p class="public-byline">
    by <%= link_to "@#{pub.handle}", public_user_path(pub.handle) %>
    <%# THE SAME RULE AS THE MAKER'S OWN PAGE (stage 4, S4-6): `planning` is the column default and
        badging a default is noise; a status the maker SET is worth a badge. Found in review: the
        status variants were restyled in components.css and this page went on badging the default. %>
    <% unless project.status == "planning" %>
      <span class="badge status-<%= project.status %>"><%= project.status.humanize %></span>
    <% end %>
    <% if project.license_label %>
      <span class="badge license"><%= project.license_label %></span>
    <% end %>
    <% if project.unlisted? %>
      <span class="badge visibility-unlisted">Unlisted link</span>
    <% end %>
    <%# NO LINK TO A FEED WITH NOTHING IN IT (hardening, 2026-09-08; tinkerex#283). The feed carries
        exactly two kinds, and a maker who hid both panels publishes neither -- so the document is
        still valid Atom with zero entries, and advertising it here would invite a reader to
        subscribe to a stream that can never say anything. EITHER panel is enough: one visible kind
        is a feed worth following. The Atom <link rel="alternate"> in public/projects/show.html.erb
        is gated on the same pair and must stay in step -- a reader finds the feed through that
        <link> as readily as through this anchor. %>
    <% if pub.panel_visible?("build_log") || pub.panel_visible?("releases") %>
      <%= link_to "Feed", public_feed_path(pub.handle, project.slug, **pub.link_params), class: "feed-link" %>
    <% end %>
  </p>

  <%# STAGE 3, S3-6: THE BUNDLE, BUILT IN THE VISITOR'S BROWSER. The button hands
      `bundle_controller.js` a manifest URL and nothing else; that controller fetches each file
      through the SAME public route this page links, adds bom.csv, and zips it client-side, so no
      byte passes through Rails that a plain download would not already have moved (§15). The
      status line is where a refused fetch is reported -- on the s3 deployment the public file
      route redirects to a presigned URL at the bucket, a cross-origin fetch that reads only
      under the bucket's CORS rule (docs/DEPLOY.md) and the connect-src the port names for the
      CSP, so the button must SAY it failed rather than sit there having done nothing.

      `bundle.any?` IS THE ONLY CONDITION AND NO PANEL GATE JOINS IT (hardening, 2026-09-08;
      tinkerex#283). The three other side documents are gated on the panel they belong to; this one
      has no panel to be hidden by, because `attachments` is in Maker::ProjectKind::ALWAYS and no
      override may hide the folder. So the empty test covers the one case that exists -- a folder
      with nothing to zip -- and a second condition here would be a branch nothing can take (the
      argument in full at Maker::PublicProject#bundle_files). The BOM inside the zip IS gated: the
      manifest's `bom_csv_url` is null when that panel is hidden and the script skips it. %>
  <% bundle = pub.bundle_files %>
  <% if bundle.any? %>
    <div class="bundle" data-controller="bundle"
         data-bundle-manifest-value="<%= public_bundle_path(pub.handle, project.slug, **pub.link_params) %>"
         data-bundle-name-value="<%= project.slug %>">
      <button type="button" class="btn small" data-action="bundle#build">
        <%# "FIRST N FILES" WHEN THE LISTING HIT ITS CAP, the same fact the manifest carries as
            `truncated`. A button that said "300 files" for a folder holding 900 would be a lie the
            visitor cannot detect until they open the zip. %>
        Download bundle (<%= "first " if pub.listing.truncated? %><%= pluralize(bundle.size, "file") %>, <%= number_to_human_size(bundle.sum(&:size)) %>)
      </button>
      <span class="muted" data-bundle-target="status"></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>

<% if project.description.present? %>
  <p class="project-description"><%= project.description %></p>
<% end %>

<%# NOT `tag_chips(project)`: that helper links into /tags/:name, which is the SIGNED-IN owner's
    private tag page. A visitor's tag link belongs on /explore. %>
<% if project.tags.any? %>
  <div class="tag-chips">
    <% project.tags.sort_by(&:name).each do |t| %>
      <%= link_to t.name, public_explore_path(tag: t.name), class: "tag-chip" %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>

<% readme = pub.readme %>
<% if readme&.html.present? %>
  <section class="panel readme-panel">
    <div class="markdown"><%= readme.html %></div>
    <% if readme.note %>
      <p class="muted readme-note"><%= readme.note %></p>
    <% end %>
  </section>
<% elsif readme&.note %>
  <p class="muted readme-note"><%= readme.note %></p>
<% end %>

<%# THE HERO (stage 4, S4-5; story 5.7): the same picture the private page has led with since S4-1,
    in the same place on it, on the page a visitor sees. An image-role cover is the picture itself
    through the public BYTE route; any other cover is its READY server thumbnail. NO POLL and NO
    placeholder box — /files/thumbnails is session-authed, so a spinner here would turn forever, and
    a page with no cover simply has no hero, exactly as the private one.

    `pub.cover_node` is the PERMITTED cover: a chosen cover is a raw column nothing validates, so an
    unpermitted one renders nothing rather than becoming the page's largest picture.
    `pub.thumbs` is already memoised for the page, so the hero costs no query of its own; a cover
    outside the listing is simply not in that map and falls through to no picture, which is the
    honest answer on a page that cannot poll. %>
<% if (cover = pub.cover_node) %>
  <div class="project-hero">
    <%= link_to pub.view_url_for(cover.node_id) do %>
      <%# `inline_image?`, NOT the role (tinkerex#285): the same split projects/_hero makes. %>
      <% if Maker::FileAttachment.inline_image?(cover.name) %>
        <%= image_tag pub.file_url_for(cover.node_id), class: "project-hero-img", alt: cover.name %>
      <% else %>
        <% cover_thumb = pub.thumbs.thumb(cover.node_id) %>
        <%# THE PUBLIC PREVIEW ROUTE, NEVER `url_for(cover_thumb.asset)` (hardening, 2026-09-08;
            tinkerex#278): a blob URL is unauthenticated and never expires, so the page's LARGEST
            picture was the one that outlived the publication longest. `pub.preview_url_for` carries
            this project's link_params, so an unlisted project's hero keeps its `k`. %>
        <% if cover_thumb&.status == "ready" && cover_thumb.asset.attached? %>
          <%= image_tag pub.preview_url_for(cover.node_id), class: "project-hero-img",
                        alt: "Preview of #{cover.name}" %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
    <span class="project-hero-name mono muted"><%= cover.name %></span>
  </div>
<% end %>

<%#
  THE PANELS, IN THE OWNER'S OWN ORDER (stage 4, S4-7; story 6.5). The visitor sees the page the
  maker arranged: a panel they hid is hidden here too, and a panel they moved is moved here too --
  one layout, read off the project through Maker::PublicProject#visible_panels, so the maker's page
  and a visitor's cannot disagree about what is hidden.

  THE MAP IS THE WHOLE DEFINITION OF "HAS A PUBLIC FACE". `tasks`, `sheets` and `publish` are
  absent from it and are therefore absent from a visitor's page WHATEVER the order says, which is
  exactly what they were before this loop existed -- there is no second list to keep in step, and a
  maker who drags Tasks to the top does not publish their task list.

  EVERY PARTIAL IS NAMED WITH ITS FULL PATH, AND MUST STAY THAT WAY -- including `repo_card` below.
  A bare `render "photos"` resolves against the CONTROLLER's prefixes, not this file's directory:
  under Public::ProjectsController that is public/projects/_photos, but under
  ProjectsController#preview it is projects/_photos, the maker's OWN panel with its composer,
  rendered into the preview with nothing going red. `files` has no private twin and would raise;
  the others would silently render the wrong page.

  WIRING IS THE ONE SPECIAL CASE and it is not new: the shared projects/_wiring partial is what the
  visitor gets, with `editable: false`, and it has always been wrapped in a check for any
  connections at all -- read-only, its empty state is a heading and nothing else, which is a panel
  rather than the absence the rest of this page uses.

  AN EMPTY PANEL IS STILL OMITTED. Each partial below decides that for itself, exactly as before:
  the layout decides whether a panel is RENDERED, the partial decides whether it has anything to
  render. Two questions, the same pair the private page keeps as hidden versus quiet.
%>
<% public_panels = { "attachments" => "public/projects/files",
                     "photos"      => "public/projects/photos",
                     "schematics"  => "public/projects/schematics",
                     "build_log"   => "public/projects/build_log",
                     "bom"         => "public/projects/bom",
                     "releases"    => "public/projects/releases" } %>
<div class="project-panels">
  <% pub.visible_panels.each do |key| %>
    <% if key == "wiring" %>
      <% if pub.connections.any? %>
        <%= render "projects/wiring", project: project, connections: pub.connections, editable: false %>
      <% end %>
    <% elsif (partial = public_panels[key]) %>
      <%= render partial, pub: pub %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
  <% pub.repo_cards.each_value do |card| %>
    <%= render "public/projects/repo_card", card: card %>
  <% end %>
</div>
